{:edb/db-before-t 2284 :edb/db-after-t 2285 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535597 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:16:52.634Z" 13194139535597 true] [17592186124232 1100 "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A)$." 13194139535597 false] [17592186124232 1100 "Let $A$ be a $4 \\times 7$ matrix that has $3$ **non-pivot** columns. Find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" 13194139535597 true] [17592186124232 1145 "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$.\n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is\n$7-3=4$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A)=4\n$$\n\n." 13194139535597 false] [17592186124232 1145 "We know that $\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A$. \n\nSince $A$ has $7$ columns and $3$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n7-3=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$." 13194139535597 true] [17592186124233 1100 "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535597 false] [17592186124233 1100 "Which of the following matrices have a rank of $4?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068273/assets/1e19a98b8ff5b436e74552c814581f016d4182e6fc552ee69273cd70bbe2f9de.png)" 13194139535597 true] [17592186124233 1145 "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows.\nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows.\n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A)=3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(C)=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"." 13194139535597 false] [17592186124233 1145 "First, notice that a matrix of rank $4$ must have at least $4$ columns and at least $4$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $B$ since it has only $3$ rows. \n\nAlso, the matrix must have $4$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $A$ has only $3$ pivot columns, while the matrix $C$ has exactly $4$ pivot columns. So, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\textrm{rank}(C)=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"." 13194139535597 true] [17592186124234 1100 "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14068273/assets/fe24ddff89c73a9a50b4b489ab3828763b68685e158a82041b1ce2be59f854d6.png), find $\\textrm{rank}(A).$" 13194139535597 true] [17592186124234 1100 "If $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$, find $\\textrm{rank}(A)$." 13194139535597 false] [17592186124234 1145 "To find the rank of $A$, we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3} &:=R_{3} + 2R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + 6R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A)=3\n$$" 13194139535597 false] [17592186124234 1145 "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 35\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has $3$ pivot columns (or, alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{rank}(A)=3.$" 13194139535597 true] [17592186124426 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356779 13194139535597 true] [17592186124426 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356786 13194139535597 true] [17592186124426 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356807 13194139535597 true] [17592186124426 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356849 13194139535597 true] [17592186124427 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356793 13194139535597 true] [17592186124427 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356800 13194139535597 true] [17592186124427 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356835 13194139535597 true] [17592186124427 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356856 13194139535597 true] [17592186124427 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356863 13194139535597 true] [17592186124428 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356814 13194139535597 true] [17592186124428 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356821 13194139535597 true] [17592186124428 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356828 13194139535597 true] [17592186124428 1020 #edb/ref 17592186356842 13194139535597 true] [17592186124773 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535597 true] [17592186124773 1100 "If $A$ is a $6 \\cdot 11$ matrix that has $8$ **non-pivot** columns, then $rank(A) =$" 13194139535597 false] [17592186124773 1100 "If $A$ is a $6\\times 11$ matrix that has $8$ **non-pivot** columns, then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" 13194139535597 true] [17592186124773 1145 "We know that $\\text{rank}(A)$ is the number of pivot columns in $A.$ \n\nSince $A$ has $11$ columns and $8$ of them are non-pivots, then the number of pivot columns in $A$ is \n\n$$\n11-8=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$" 13194139535597 true] [17592186124791 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535597 true] [17592186124791 1100 "If\n\n$H = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$, then $rank(H) =$" 13194139535597 false] [17592186124791 1100 "If \n$H=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$, then $\\text{rank}(H)=$" 13194139535597 true] [17592186124791 1145 "To find the rank of $H,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(H)=2.$" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356779 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356780 13194139535597 true] [17592186356779 1089 "q-139950" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356779 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535597 true] [17592186356779 1100 "Let $T$ be a $8\\times 8$ matrix that has $7$ **pivot** columns. What is the rank of the matrix?" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356779 1113 #uuid "0fa05281-2433-5107-8bfa-eafaa67c6a39" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356779 1145 "We know that $\\text{rank}(T)$ is the number of pivot columns in $T.$ \n\nSince we are told that $T$ has $7$ pivot columns, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=7.\n$$" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356781 13194139535597 true] [17592186356780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356782 13194139535597 true] [17592186356780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356783 13194139535597 true] [17592186356780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356784 13194139535597 true] [17592186356780 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356785 13194139535597 true] [17592186356780 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535597 true] [17592186356780 1041 "selection" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356780 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356782 13194139535597 true] [17592186356780 1110 #uuid "8f2a2e05-d7d4-56bf-8852-790307fecd78" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356781 1034 "8" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356781 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535597 true] [17592186356781 1111 #uuid "c72f6881-f80b-5cd9-a864-8bb1d2d34405" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356782 1034 "7" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535597 true] [17592186356782 1111 #uuid "0404557c-351b-5f64-9073-a1a26dffec7d" 13194139535597 true] [17592186356783 1034 "6" 13194139535597 true] [1759